/* ============================================================
   SECTION 3: ENCAPSULATION & PROPERTIES
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">3. Encapsulation & Properties</h2>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Encapsulation</strong> is the practice of hiding internal state and requiring all interaction through methods. It protects data integrity — you control <em>how</em> attributes are accessed and modified. Python\'s approach is unique: it relies on <strong>conventions</strong> rather than strict access modifiers like Java\'s <code>private</code>/<code>protected</code> keywords.</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-steps">'
+ '<div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">_</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Single underscore prefix</strong> — <code>self._internal</code>: Convention for "private" attributes. Not enforced by Python, but signals to other developers: "this is internal, do not access directly." Importable but not included in <code>from module import *</code>.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">__</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Double underscore prefix</strong> — <code>self.__secret</code>: Triggers <strong>name mangling</strong>. Python renames it to <code>_ClassName__secret</code> to prevent accidental override in subclasses. Not true "private" — you can still access it with the mangled name.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">__x__</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Dunder (double underscore both sides)</strong> — <code>__init__</code>, <code>__str__</code>: Reserved for Python\'s special methods. Never invent your own dunder names — they are part of the language protocol.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">class</span> <span class="fn">BankAccount</span>:\n    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">__init__</span>(self, owner, balance=<span class="num">0</span>):\n        self.owner = owner          <span class="cm"># public</span>\n        self._transactions = []     <span class="cm"># "private" by convention</span>\n        self.__balance = balance     <span class="cm"># name-mangled</span>\n\n    @<span class="fn">property</span>\n    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">balance</span>(self):\n        <span class="str">"""Read-only access to balance."""</span>\n        <span class="kw">return</span> self.__balance\n\n    @balance.setter\n    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">balance</span>(self, value):\n        <span class="kw">if</span> value < <span class="num">0</span>:\n            <span class="kw">raise</span> <span class="fn">ValueError</span>(<span class="str">"Balance cannot be negative"</span>)\n        self.__balance = value\n\n    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">deposit</span>(self, amount):\n        <span class="kw">if</span> amount <= <span class="num">0</span>:\n            <span class="kw">raise</span> <span class="fn">ValueError</span>(<span class="str">"Deposit must be positive"</span>)\n        self.__balance += amount\n        self._transactions.<span class="fn">append</span>((<span class="str">"deposit"</span>, amount))\n\n    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">withdraw</span>(self, amount):\n        <span class="kw">if</span> amount > self.__balance:\n            <span class="kw">raise</span> <span class="fn">ValueError</span>(<span class="str">"Insufficient funds"</span>)\n        self.__balance -= amount\n        self._transactions.<span class="fn">append</span>((<span class="str">"withdraw"</span>, amount))\n\naccount = <span class="fn">BankAccount</span>(<span class="str">"Alice"</span>, <span class="num">1000</span>)\naccount.<span class="fn">deposit</span>(<span class="num">500</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(account.balance)     <span class="cm"># 1500 (uses @property getter)</span>\naccount.balance = <span class="num">2000</span>      <span class="cm"># uses @balance.setter</span>\n<span class="cm"># account.balance = -100    # ValueError: Balance cannot be negative</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Name mangling in action</span>\n<span class="cm"># account.__balance         # AttributeError!</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(account._BankAccount__balance)  <span class="cm"># 2000 (mangled name)</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="calc-compare"><div class="compare-col"><div class="compare-title">PYTHON APPROACH</div><div class="compare-item">&bull; Convention over enforcement</div><div class="compare-item">&bull; <code>_private</code> = "please don\'t touch"</div><div class="compare-item">&bull; <code>@property</code> for controlled access</div><div class="compare-item">&bull; Name mangling for subclass safety</div><div class="compare-item">&bull; "We are all consenting adults"</div></div><div class="compare-col"><div class="compare-title">JAVA APPROACH</div><div class="compare-item">&bull; Strict compiler enforcement</div><div class="compare-item">&bull; <code>private</code> keyword = truly hidden</div><div class="compare-item">&bull; Explicit getter/setter methods</div><div class="compare-item">&bull; <code>protected</code> for subclass access</div><div class="compare-item">&bull; Access modifiers are mandatory</div></div></div>'

+ '<div class="think-box"><div class="think-label">Think about it</div><div class="think-body">Python\'s <code>@property</code> decorator is a game-changer. It lets you start with simple public attributes (<code>self.x = 5</code>) and later add validation, computation, or logging <em>without changing the API</em>. Callers still write <code>obj.x</code> — they never know a property is involved. This is why the Python philosophy is: "start with simple attributes, add properties only when you need them."</div></div>'

/* ============================================================
   SECTION 4: MAGIC METHODS (DUNDER)
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">4. Magic Methods (Dunder)</h2>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Magic methods</strong> (also called dunder methods, for "double underscore") are Python\'s way of letting your custom classes integrate seamlessly with built-in operations. When you write <code>a + b</code>, Python calls <code>a.__add__(b)</code>. When you write <code>len(x)</code>, Python calls <code>x.__len__()</code>. By implementing these methods, your classes can behave like built-in types — they can be added, iterated, sliced, hashed, and more.</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-cards">'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">__add__ / __radd__</div><div class="card-body"><code>a + b</code> calls <code>a.__add__(b)</code>. If NotImplemented, tries <code>b.__radd__(a)</code>.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">__len__</div><div class="card-body"><code>len(obj)</code> calls <code>obj.__len__()</code>. Must return non-negative int.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">__getitem__</div><div class="card-body"><code>obj[key]</code> calls <code>obj.__getitem__(key)</code>. Enables indexing and slicing.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">__iter__ / __next__</div><div class="card-body">Makes object iterable. <code>for x in obj</code> calls <code>__iter__</code>, then <code>__next__</code> repeatedly.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">__eq__ / __hash__</div><div class="card-body"><code>a == b</code> calls <code>__eq__</code>. If you define <code>__eq__</code>, define <code>__hash__</code> too for dict/set use.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">__lt__ / __le__ / __gt__</div><div class="card-body">Comparison operators. Implement <code>__lt__</code> and <code>__eq__</code>, use <code>@functools.total_ordering</code> for the rest.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">__enter__ / __exit__</div><div class="card-body">Context manager protocol. <code>with obj as x:</code> calls <code>__enter__</code>, cleanup via <code>__exit__</code>.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">__call__</div><div class="card-body"><code>obj()</code> calls <code>obj.__call__()</code>. Makes instances callable like functions.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">__bool__ / __contains__</div><div class="card-body"><code>bool(obj)</code> and <code>x in obj</code>. Control truthiness and membership testing.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">__del__</div><div class="card-body">Destructor — called when object is garbage collected. Rarely needed; prefer context managers.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">__getattr__ / __setattr__</div><div class="card-body">Intercept attribute access/assignment. Powerful for proxies, ORMs, and dynamic objects.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">__format__</div><div class="card-body"><code>f"{obj:.2f}"</code> calls <code>obj.__format__(".2f")</code>. Customize string formatting.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

/* Full Vector2D example */
+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Building a complete class with dunders:</strong> Let\'s implement a <code>Vector2D</code> class that supports arithmetic, comparison, iteration, and more:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> math\n\n<span class="kw">class</span> <span class="fn">Vector2D</span>:\n    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">__init__</span>(self, x, y):\n        self.x = x\n        self.y = y\n\n    <span class="cm"># --- String representations ---</span>\n    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">__repr__</span>(self):\n        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="str">f"Vector2D(</span>{self.x}<span class="str">, </span>{self.y}<span class="str">)"</span>\n\n    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">__str__</span>(self):\n        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="str">f"(</span>{self.x}<span class="str">, </span>{self.y}<span class="str">)"</span>\n\n    <span class="cm"># --- Arithmetic ---</span>\n    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">__add__</span>(self, other):\n        <span class="kw">if</span> <span class="fn">isinstance</span>(other, Vector2D):\n            <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="fn">Vector2D</span>(self.x + other.x, self.y + other.y)\n        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="kw">NotImplemented</span>\n\n    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">__mul__</span>(self, scalar):\n        <span class="kw">if</span> <span class="fn">isinstance</span>(scalar, (<span class="fn">int</span>, <span class="fn">float</span>)):\n            <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="fn">Vector2D</span>(self.x * scalar, self.y * scalar)\n        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="kw">NotImplemented</span>\n\n    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">__rmul__</span>(self, scalar):  <span class="cm"># 3 * vec</span>\n        <span class="kw">return</span> self.<span class="fn">__mul__</span>(scalar)\n\n    <span class="cm"># --- Length & magnitude ---</span>\n    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">__abs__</span>(self):  <span class="cm"># abs(vec) = magnitude</span>\n        <span class="kw">return</span> math.<span class="fn">hypot</span>(self.x, self.y)\n\n    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">__len__</span>(self):  <span class="cm"># always 2 components</span>\n        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="num">2</span>\n\n    <span class="cm"># --- Indexing & iteration ---</span>\n    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">__getitem__</span>(self, index):\n        <span class="kw">if</span> index == <span class="num">0</span>: <span class="kw">return</span> self.x\n        <span class="kw">if</span> index == <span class="num">1</span>: <span class="kw">return</span> self.y\n        <span class="kw">raise</span> <span class="fn">IndexError</span>(<span class="str">"Vector2D has only 2 components"</span>)\n\n    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">__iter__</span>(self):\n        <span class="kw">yield</span> self.x\n        <span class="kw">yield</span> self.y\n\n    <span class="cm"># --- Comparison & hashing ---</span>\n    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">__eq__</span>(self, other):\n        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="fn">isinstance</span>(other, Vector2D) <span class="kw">and</span> self.x == other.x <span class="kw">and</span> self.y == other.y\n\n    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">__hash__</span>(self):\n        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="fn">hash</span>((self.x, self.y))\n\n    <span class="cm"># --- Boolean ---</span>\n    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">__bool__</span>(self):  <span class="cm"># zero vector is falsy</span>\n        <span class="kw">return</span> self.x != <span class="num">0</span> <span class="kw">or</span> self.y != <span class="num">0</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Usage</span>\nv1 = <span class="fn">Vector2D</span>(<span class="num">3</span>, <span class="num">4</span>)\nv2 = <span class="fn">Vector2D</span>(<span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">2</span>)\n\n<span class="fn">print</span>(v1 + v2)       <span class="cm"># (4, 6)</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(v1 * <span class="num">3</span>)        <span class="cm"># (9, 12)</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="num">2</span> * v2)         <span class="cm"># (2, 4) — uses __rmul__</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="fn">abs</span>(v1))        <span class="cm"># 5.0 (magnitude)</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(v1[<span class="num">0</span>], v1[<span class="num">1</span>])  <span class="cm"># 3 4</span>\nx, y = v1            <span class="cm"># unpacking via __iter__</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(v1 == <span class="fn">Vector2D</span>(<span class="num">3</span>, <span class="num">4</span>))  <span class="cm"># True</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>({v1: <span class="str">"origin"</span>})  <span class="cm"># usable as dict key</span></code></pre></div>'

