+ '<div class="calc-example"><div class="example-label">Context Manager Example</div><div class="example-body"><p class="l-text">The <code>__enter__</code>/<code>__exit__</code> protocol powers the <code>with</code> statement — Python\'s elegant way to manage resources:</p><div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">class</span> <span class="fn">Timer</span>:\n    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">__init__</span>(self, label=<span class="str">"Block"</span>):\n        self.label = label\n\n    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">__enter__</span>(self):\n        <span class="kw">import</span> time\n        self.start = time.<span class="fn">perf_counter</span>()\n        <span class="kw">return</span> self  <span class="cm"># bound to "as" variable</span>\n\n    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">__exit__</span>(self, exc_type, exc_val, exc_tb):\n        <span class="kw">import</span> time\n        elapsed = time.<span class="fn">perf_counter</span>() - self.start\n        <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"</span>{self.label}<span class="str">: </span>{elapsed:<span class="str">.4f}s"</span>)\n        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="kw">False</span>  <span class="cm"># don\'t suppress exceptions</span>\n\n<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">Timer</span>(<span class="str">"Sorting"</span>):\n    <span class="fn">sorted</span>(<span class="fn">range</span>(<span class="num">1000000</span>), reverse=<span class="kw">True</span>)\n<span class="cm"># Output: Sorting: 0.0821s</span></code></pre></div></div></div>'

/* ============================================================
   SECTION 5: DECORATORS & METACLASSES
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">5. Decorators & Metaclasses</h2>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Decorators</strong> are one of Python\'s most elegant features. A decorator is simply a function that takes another function (or class) as input and returns a modified version of it. The <code>@decorator</code> syntax is syntactic sugar: <code>@deco</code> above a function <code>f</code> is equivalent to <code>f = deco(f)</code>.</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-steps">'
+ '<div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">1</div><div class="step-content"><strong>@staticmethod</strong> — A method that does not receive <code>self</code> or <code>cls</code>. It is just a regular function that lives inside the class namespace for organizational purposes. Use for utility functions related to the class.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">2</div><div class="step-content"><strong>@classmethod</strong> — Receives the class (<code>cls</code>) as first argument instead of the instance. Use for alternative constructors (<code>from_string</code>, <code>from_json</code>) and factory methods.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">3</div><div class="step-content"><strong>@property</strong> — Turns a method into a read-only attribute. Combined with <code>@x.setter</code> and <code>@x.deleter</code> for full control. Already covered in Section 3.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">class</span> <span class="fn">Date</span>:\n    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">__init__</span>(self, year, month, day):\n        self.year = year\n        self.month = month\n        self.day = day\n\n    @<span class="fn">classmethod</span>\n    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">from_string</span>(cls, date_str):\n        <span class="str">"""Alternative constructor: Date.from_string(\'2024-01-15\')"""</span>\n        year, month, day = <span class="fn">map</span>(<span class="fn">int</span>, date_str.<span class="fn">split</span>(<span class="str">"-"</span>))\n        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="fn">cls</span>(year, month, day)  <span class="cm"># cls, not Date!</span>\n\n    @<span class="fn">classmethod</span>\n    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">today</span>(cls):\n        <span class="kw">import</span> datetime\n        t = datetime.date.<span class="fn">today</span>()\n        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="fn">cls</span>(t.year, t.month, t.day)\n\n    @<span class="fn">staticmethod</span>\n    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">is_valid</span>(year, month, day):\n        <span class="str">"""Utility: no self or cls needed."""</span>\n        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="num">1</span> <= month <= <span class="num">12</span> <span class="kw">and</span> <span class="num">1</span> <= day <= <span class="num">31</span>\n\n    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">__str__</span>(self):\n        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="str">f"</span>{self.year}<span class="str">-</span>{self.month:02d}<span class="str">-</span>{self.day:02d}<span class="str">"</span>\n\nd1 = <span class="fn">Date</span>(<span class="num">2024</span>, <span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">15</span>)\nd2 = Date.<span class="fn">from_string</span>(<span class="str">"2024-06-20"</span>)  <span class="cm"># classmethod constructor</span>\nd3 = Date.<span class="fn">today</span>()                     <span class="cm"># classmethod constructor</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(Date.<span class="fn">is_valid</span>(<span class="num">2024</span>, <span class="num">13</span>, <span class="num">1</span>))     <span class="cm"># False (staticmethod)</span></code></pre></div>'

/* Custom Decorators */
+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Writing custom decorators</strong> unlocks powerful patterns — logging, timing, caching, authentication, and more. Here are two practical examples:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> functools\n<span class="kw">import</span> time\n\n<span class="cm"># --- Timing decorator ---</span>\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">timer</span>(func):\n    @functools.<span class="fn">wraps</span>(func)  <span class="cm"># preserve original name/docstring</span>\n    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">wrapper</span>(*args, **kwargs):\n        start = time.<span class="fn">perf_counter</span>()\n        result = <span class="fn">func</span>(*args, **kwargs)\n        elapsed = time.<span class="fn">perf_counter</span>() - start\n        <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"</span>{func.__name__}<span class="str">() took </span>{elapsed:<span class="str">.4f}s"</span>)\n        <span class="kw">return</span> result\n    <span class="kw">return</span> wrapper\n\n@timer\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">process_data</span>(n):\n    <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="fn">sum</span>(i**<span class="num">2</span> <span class="kw">for</span> i <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="fn">range</span>(n))\n\n<span class="fn">process_data</span>(<span class="num">1000000</span>)  <span class="cm"># process_data() took 0.1234s</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># --- Retry decorator with arguments ---</span>\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">retry</span>(max_attempts=<span class="num">3</span>, delay=<span class="num">1.0</span>):\n    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">decorator</span>(func):\n        @functools.<span class="fn">wraps</span>(func)\n        <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">wrapper</span>(*args, **kwargs):\n            <span class="kw">for</span> attempt <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="fn">range</span>(<span class="num">1</span>, max_attempts + <span class="num">1</span>):\n                <span class="kw">try</span>:\n                    <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="fn">func</span>(*args, **kwargs)\n                <span class="kw">except</span> <span class="fn">Exception</span> <span class="kw">as</span> e:\n                    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Attempt </span>{attempt}<span class="str"> failed: </span>{e}<span class="str">"</span>)\n                    <span class="kw">if</span> attempt < max_attempts:\n                        time.<span class="fn">sleep</span>(delay)\n            <span class="kw">raise</span> <span class="fn">Exception</span>(<span class="str">f"Failed after </span>{max_attempts}<span class="str"> attempts"</span>)\n        <span class="kw">return</span> wrapper\n    <span class="kw">return</span> decorator\n\n@<span class="fn">retry</span>(max_attempts=<span class="num">3</span>, delay=<span class="num">0.5</span>)\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">fetch_data</span>(url):\n    <span class="kw">import</span> random\n    <span class="kw">if</span> random.<span class="fn">random</span>() < <span class="num">0.7</span>:\n        <span class="kw">raise</span> <span class="fn">ConnectionError</span>(<span class="str">"Server down"</span>)\n    <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="str">"Data received"</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="l-note"><strong>functools.wraps is essential:</strong> Without it, the decorated function loses its original <code>__name__</code>, <code>__doc__</code>, and <code>__module__</code>. The <code>@functools.wraps(func)</code> decorator copies these attributes to the wrapper, so introspection tools, debuggers, and documentation generators still work correctly.</div>'

/* Metaclasses */
+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Metaclasses</strong> are the "classes of classes." While a class defines how instances behave, a metaclass defines how <em>classes</em> behave. The default metaclass is <code>type</code>. Metaclasses are advanced and rarely needed — Django\'s ORM and SQLAlchemy use them under the hood, but most developers never write one.</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># Every class is an instance of type</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="fn">type</span>(<span class="fn">int</span>))     <span class="cm"># &lt;class \'type\'&gt;</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="fn">type</span>(<span class="fn">str</span>))     <span class="cm"># &lt;class \'type\'&gt;</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="fn">type</span>(<span class="fn">type</span>))    <span class="cm"># &lt;class \'type\'&gt; (type is its own metaclass!)</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Simple metaclass example: auto-register subclasses</span>\n<span class="kw">class</span> <span class="fn">PluginMeta</span>(<span class="fn">type</span>):\n    registry = {}\n\n    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">__new__</span>(mcs, name, bases, namespace):\n        cls = <span class="fn">super</span>().<span class="fn">__new__</span>(mcs, name, bases, namespace)\n        <span class="kw">if</span> bases:  <span class="cm"># don\'t register the base class itself</span>\n            PluginMeta.registry[name] = cls\n        <span class="kw">return</span> cls\n\n<span class="kw">class</span> <span class="fn">Plugin</span>(metaclass=PluginMeta):\n    <span class="kw">pass</span>\n\n<span class="kw">class</span> <span class="fn">AudioPlugin</span>(Plugin):\n    <span class="kw">pass</span>\n\n<span class="kw">class</span> <span class="fn">VideoPlugin</span>(Plugin):\n    <span class="kw">pass</span>\n\n<span class="fn">print</span>(PluginMeta.registry)\n<span class="cm"># {\'AudioPlugin\': &lt;class \'AudioPlugin\'&gt;, \'VideoPlugin\': &lt;class \'VideoPlugin\'&gt;}</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="think-box"><div class="think-label">Think about it</div><div class="think-body">Tim Peters (author of the Zen of Python) famously said: "Metaclasses are deeper magic than 99% of users should ever worry about." In practice, <code>__init_subclass__</code> (Python 3.6+) and class decorators solve most problems that used to require metaclasses. Prefer the simpler tool unless you truly need to intercept class creation at the lowest level.</div></div>'

